 {"id":"fabro-c49c","title":"Close the gate-ban equivalence loophole in planner step 7","status":"closed","type":"task","priority":1,"createdAt":"2026-09-09T20:14:39.400Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-10T08:34:43.824Z","description":"In .fabro/workflows/develop/prompts/planner.md step 7, extend the gate-command rewrite rule from matching only 'just qualitygate' to also matching its recipe body or equivalent invocation (per PROJECT_FACTS the recipe delegates to 'nu scripts/qualitygate.nu'); the permitted substitute stays gate-green via the deterministic tester step. In run 01M23W4S7Q2GNEVPY779M4GMN6 the planner rewrote the banned command into its byte-equivalent body (checkpoint seq 82, justfile:128), so the implementer and reviewer each ran the full gate on top of the tester — three gate executions on one tree. The prior fix (fabro-f7cf, closed) only matched the literal command name; this is the bypass of that implemented rule, a different mechanism. Expected effect: no redundant full-gate runs; on Rust-touching seeds each avoided run is a cold compile measured at 8-15 min. Cross-references: fabro-f7cf (prior, implemented). Basis: run 01M23W4S7Q2GNEVPY779M4GMN6, workflow version 38bab0b8b1faad62aaddb5e68facf6f1c58286169676479d3edbf99d4205b91c, commit 4c8683243f8c295438cf019e2f061f8812fc58db","labels":["revision"],"assignee":"fabro","closedAt":"2026-09-10T08:34:43.824Z"}
 {"id":"fabro-5e3c","title":"Fix hardcoded cwd restore in evidence-smoke.nu","status":"open","type":"task","priority":2,"createdAt":"2026-09-09T20:14:45.326Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-09T20:22:22.837Z","description":"In scripts/evidence-smoke.nu (~line 60), replace the hardcoded 'cd /workspace/fabro' cwd restore with a captured $env.PWD at script start. The reviewer in run 01M23W4S7Q2GNEVPY779M4GMN6 found this defect (journal seq 296) and approved anyway because the only channel was a full Changes-requested cycle; the smoke was checked into the gate by seed fabro-bfe1's new loop-asset tier and now executes in every future 'just qualitygate', misbehaving in any worktree checkout. Expected effect: the new gate tier stays green outside the canonical checkout path, and the latent break stops executing on every run. Cross-references: fabro-bfe1 (open — added the tier that checked this script into the gate; thematic overlap, not the same change). Basis: run 01M23W4S7Q2GNEVPY779M4GMN6, workflow version 38bab0b8b1faad62aaddb5e68facf6f1c58286169676479d3edbf99d4205b91c, commit 4c8683243f8c295438cf019e2f061f8812fc58db","labels":["revision"],"assignee":"fabro"}
 {"id":"fabro-afb8","title":"smoke.nu uses 'get -o' which nushell 0.101 rejects — weblab deploys fail the smoke recipe although the server is healthy","status":"closed","type":"bug","priority":1,"createdAt":"2026-09-09T20:32:25.962Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-10T08:28:33.428Z","assignee":"fabro","description":"Weblab deploy 2026-09-09 ~20:40Z: 'just up' completed fully (image built, CLI 0.348.0 installed, compose recreated fabro-fabro-1, wait-healthy green twice) but the recipe then FAILED at scripts/smoke.nu:91 with a PARSE error: 'The get command doesn't have flag -o' on 'open $auth_path | get -o servers | default {}'. The weblab HOST nushell is 0.101.0 (just invokes the host nu; the mise nushell 0.115.0 pin lives only in the run toolchain image) while the dev machine that authored/tested smoke.nu runs nu 0.115.0, where get -o/--optional exists. Result: every weblab deploy reports failure while the deployment actually succeeded — the deploy ceremony cannot trust its exit code. Wanted: make smoke.nu (and audit the other nu deploy scripts: wait-healthy.nu, install-cli.nu) compatible with the OLDEST supported host nu, or have the justfile invoke the pinned nushell explicitly. 0.101-compatible form for the failing line: 'get -i servers | default {}' (-i/--ignore-errors exists in 0.101) or a try/catch wrapper. Acceptance: 'nu --ide-check'-level parse of smoke.nu under 0.101 semantics (or a smoke run against a 0.101 host); deploy on weblab exits 0. Basis: weblab ~/deploy-20260909.log tail (parse error + 'Recipe smoke failed'), weblab nu --version 0.101.0, dev-machine nu 0.115.0, manual smoke after the failure (ps ok, auth probe 200, container healthy).","labels":["workflows"],"closedAt":"2026-09-10T08:28:33.428Z"}
-{"id":"fabro-3fe4","title":"Engine: own the run-lifecycle event protocol — one module + one shared Event-to-Status transition table","status":"open","type":"task","priority":1,"createdAt":"2026-09-10T07:14:04.557Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-10T10:13:59.658Z","description":"Architecture review 2026-09-10 (improve-codebase-architecture, user + agent; walkers: fabro-server, fabro-store/fabro-workflow). Top recommendation, filed unassigned per ADR-0018.\n\nPROBLEM: The run-lifecycle event protocol (RunStartRequested -> RunRunnable -> RunStarting -> Running, terminal fails) has no owner. Emitters are spread over operations/start.rs:137-227, operations/resume.rs:36-46, server.rs:3405-3488 (writes terminal events directly), and test_support.rs mark_run_running:112-130 (a second protocol adapter inside tests). The same event stream is folded by TWO reducers with no shared table: server.rs update_live_run_from_event:3576-3740 (no transition validation, RunRunnable suppression, publish-blocked error field) vs fabro-store run_state.rs RunProjectionReducer (try_apply_status + InvalidTransition). The Paused{prior_block}/RunUnblocked arms already drift (server.rs:3610-3616 vs run_state.rs:197-205), and run_state.rs:84-112 patches historic Submitted->Starting / RunFailed fast-forward forms because the protocol was never an interface.\n\nWANTED: (1) New fork-owned module fabro-workflow/src/operations/lifecycle_events.rs exposing the sequence as an interface (request_start / runnable / starting / running / resubmit / terminal_failure); server.rs and test_support become adapters. (2) One pure transition table (RunStatus x EventBody) -> Result<RunStatus, InvalidTransition> in fabro-types; BOTH reducers consume it, the live reducer adds only its side effects (map cleanup, bus cleanup).\n\nDRIFT RULES (fork of fabro-sh/fabro, upstream merged regularly — conductor pass): the module and table are additive/fork-owned; edits concentrate in already-fork-modified files (server.rs +279, start.rs +107, run_state.rs +34 fork lines); resume.rs is upstream-identical (churn 5/12w) and gets a minimal adapter edit only. No wire-contract change; no new pub items on AppState. Explicitly OUT OF SCOPE: the RunSupervisor full build-out (27 lock sites, ManagedRun) — that is phase 2 behind this seed.\n\nACCEPTANCE: equivalence test proving both reducers yield the same status for every event sequence (property test over the table); unit tests per transition incl. cancel-before-starting and failure-before-running; historic shims in run_state.rs carry a documented reason; lifecycle tests run without AppState lock surgery.\n\nBASIS: commit bfbe3a6c8 (2026-09-09); walk reports /tmp/arch-walk-server.md (F1/F2), /tmp/arch-walk-store-workflow.md (F2/F8); upstream churn 12w: server.rs 49, start.rs 47, run_state.rs 53, resume.rs 5.","labels":["engine","architecture"],"assignee":"fabro"}
